meantime pets are a beloved part of  anyone's family but as a victim escapes  an abuser that pet can get left behind  bianca holman shares a new program at  safeness to keep furry friends  close by safeness is a haven for those  seeking refuge from domestic violence in  clark county  now the non-profit is able to do more  safeness expanded shelter services to  include on-site pet boarding  for cats and dogs and the idea of you  know uprooting your children and getting  them into shelter well be it the safe  place  having to leave that pet behind is just  an another unbearable thing that that  children may have to leave behind  almost 65 percent of domestic violence  victims are unable to leave their abuser  because they're concerned about what  will happen to their pet  when they leave that's according to safe  place for pets  and 71 percent of victims report their  pets are also being abused  oftentimes the batterer will use the pet  as a manipulation tactic  threatening to kill the pet if the  significant other leaves  having the family dog or cat in the  shelter has an impact on the victim  seeing clients connect around a pet and  be in the pet area and start chatting  together and kids start playing together  and animals start playing together  it's just the right environment for  healing the pet program  includes two stand-alone housing units  with air conditioner and heat  with several dog and cat kennels safene  has also installed a shaded fenced  dog play area let's create an  environment where those fleeing domestic  violence  have as many of the barriers removed for  that as possible because  they deserve to be able to have a path  that's filled with  options and dignity just like anybody  else bianca holman  eight news now well safe nest is hosting  a summer event on july 24th the soiree  will help raise money for future  programming
 

